1
00:00:00,601 --> 00:00:03,851
I read the AI Transformation Owner job description.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:10,317
Your issue is uneven AI adoption: scattered
requests, agents without owners, priorities churning.

3
00:00:10,467 --> 00:00:16,917
I've solved this before: product ops at Corgi, and
an AI-agent operating system for my own company.

4
00:00:17,067 --> 00:00:21,984
So I built the system the AI Transformation
Owner runs. It's working, today.

5
00:00:22,134 --> 00:00:28,383
Every request moves Raise → Triage → Scout →
Deliver → Share, the flow in GitLab’s own handbook.

6
00:00:28,533 --> 00:00:34,419
Up top: hours on the table, agents with
no owner, evals passing and weekly AI use.

7
00:00:34,569 --> 00:00:40,533
And the problem itself, in one chart: weekly
AI use runs from 25% to 91% by sub-team.

8
00:00:40,633 --> 00:00:45,549
Intake: anyone in Product & Design
describes a pain point in plain language.

9
00:00:45,699 --> 00:00:52,309
No template. Numbers help, and if they’re
missing the agent estimates and says so.

10
00:00:52,459 --> 00:00:58,243
The triage agent reads it live. First, the
workflow it touches, with the evidence quoted.

11
00:00:58,393 --> 00:01:04,844
Then the 100x potential: volume × frequency × pain,
each traced to the request or a labelled prior.

12
00:01:04,994 --> 00:01:09,844
Effort checks the skills library and
the fleet first: consume before build.

13
00:01:09,994 --> 00:01:15,111
Then it groups the theme, weighs it
against this half’s outcomes, and ranks it.

14
00:01:15,261 --> 00:01:19,338
Last, a two-sentence rationale an executive can read.

15
00:01:24,700 --> 00:01:30,681
The backlog: every request ranked, labelled
the GitLab way, with its rationale inline.

16
00:01:30,831 --> 00:01:45,521
Open any item and the whole reasoning
trace is there. Nothing is a black box.

17
00:01:45,671 --> 00:01:52,904
Board by tier: Now, Next, Later and Park,
with the hours each tier would return.

18
00:01:53,054 --> 00:01:59,467
Broken tools and access requests skip the queue
and route straight to the Enterprise AI Hub.

19
00:01:59,567 --> 00:02:07,508
The agent fleet: every agent with its
owner, KPIs, eval and last-run health.

20
00:02:07,658 --> 00:02:14,276
Three agents have no owner. Until
they do, I'm accountable for them.

21
00:02:14,426 --> 00:02:20,677
This one closes issues on its own, with no owner
and no eval. That's the risk this view catches.

22
00:02:20,827 --> 00:02:25,211
Assign an owner from the sub-team, and the alert updates.

23
00:02:31,867 --> 00:02:37,184
The Champion hub: one Champion per sub-team,
at five to ten percent of their time.

24
00:02:37,334 --> 00:02:43,451
Cadence follows GitLab's operating rhythm:
fortnightly syncs, office hours and a monthly demo.

25
00:02:43,601 --> 00:02:51,694
The builder spectrum shows where each team
is, and where my coaching time goes first.

26
00:02:51,844 --> 00:02:56,376
Gaps are shown, not hidden: two
sub-teams still need a Champion.

27
00:02:56,526 --> 00:03:01,910
And the demo wall: what teams shipped,
with the number that proves it.

28
00:03:02,200 --> 00:03:08,048
The skills library: the instruction sets
agents run on. Shared, versioned, owned.

29
00:03:08,198 --> 00:03:14,928
Every skill has an owner, a version history
and the five-question promotion checklist.

30
00:03:15,078 --> 00:03:20,616
Copy, link or download it, so the
whole function runs on the same skill.

31
00:03:21,734 --> 00:03:27,651
Intake, triage, fleet and adoption: the AI
Transformation Owner's job, as working software.

32
00:03:27,801 --> 00:03:32,733
Built for GitLab Product & Design.
Open the link and try it yourself.
